{%- import "python/_types.jinja" as types -%}
{#-
  One table becomes one module: the row, its primary key, the closed set of
  columns an ordering may name, and the filter a search takes.

  Rendered once per table, per FR-RND-002 and FR-EX-005:

      tpl -d <schema> render python/dataclass --table <name> > datalayer/<schema>/<name>.py

  The file carries no timestamp. `now` is the single documented source of
  non-reproducibility, per FR-CTX-030, and a generated file that carries one
  produces a diff on every run for a reason that is not a change. Provenance
  worth recording — the server, its series, its standing and the version of
  `tpl` — is rendered once per schema by `python/core.jinja` and
  `python/package.jinja`.

  Every piece of catalogue text below reaches the file as a `#` comment and
  never as a docstring. A comment filter prefixes **every** line, per
  FR-ENV-038, so a newline inside a table comment or a generated column's
  expression produces another comment line; the same text inside a docstring
  could carry a `"""` and end it.
-#}
{%- if not table.primary_key -%}
{{- fail("python/dataclass.jinja cannot render " ~ database.name ~ "." ~ table.name ~ ": the table declares no primary key, so read, update and delete have no key to address a row by. Give the table a primary key, or render it with a template that generates a read-only data layer.") -}}
{%- endif -%}
{%- set model = types.type_name(table.name) -%}
{%- set qualified = database.name ~ "." ~ table.name -%}
{#- Which standard-library modules the mapped types need. The type mapping is
    the only thing that knows, so the answer is read off it rather than
    restated here. -#}
{%- set mapped -%}
{%- for column in table.columns %}{{ types.value_type(column) }}
{% endfor -%}
{%- endset -%}
